# Every value is an ANSI index 0-15, never 16-255 and never hex. That is the
# whole point: p10k emits \e[38;5;N for N<16, the terminal resolves N through
# its current palette, and the prompt therefore repaints on a light/dark flip
# in shells that were already open. The original .p10k.zsh used 129 indices in
# the 16-255 range, which are fixed colours that ignore the palette.
#
# Sourced AFTER .p10k.zsh, which opens with `unset -m 'POWERLEVEL9K_*'`.
#
# Verify with:
#   grep -oE '=[0-9]+$' ~/.config/zsh/p10k-colors.zsh | tr -d '=' | awk '$1>15'
# which must print nothing.

